      "severity": "high",
      "file_path": "pages.py",
      "line_start": 2,
      "line_end": 2,
      "title": "Ceiling division replaced with floor division — pages undercounted when total is not evenly divisible by size",
      "explanation": "The original formula `(total + size - 1) // size` is the standard integer ceiling-division idiom, computing `ceil(total / size)`. This is the correct way to determine how many pages of capacity `size` are needed to hold `total` items. The replacement `total // size` performs floor division, which silently drops the remainder. Whenever `total % size != 0`, the function now returns one fewer page than actually required. For example, `pages(10, 3)` previously returned 4 (correct: 3+3+3+1), but now returns 3, meaning the last item would have no page allocated. The causal chain is: (1) caller requests page count for a total that is not a multiple of size, (2) floor division truncates the remainder, (3) the returned page count is too low, (4) downstream logic (pagination display, data chunking, etc.) will miss the final partial page of items.",
      "suggestion": "Restore the ceiling-division formula: `return (total + size - 1) // size`, or equivalently use `math.ceil(total / size)` if floating-point division is acceptable. If floor division is intentionally desired for some callers, rename the function to make the semantics explicit and add a separate ceiling-division helper.",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "trigger_condition": "total % size != 0 (i.e., total is not evenly divisible by size, and both total and size are positive integers)",
      "evidence_snippet": "    return total // size",
